.\" partyline(1) — man page. Rendered by `ptln man`; installed to man1 by the deb/rpm/cask.
.TH PARTYLINE 1 "2026-10-17" "partyline" "partyline manual"
.SH NAME
partyline, ptln \- manage AI coding sessions, and keep what the team learns about a repo in the repo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ptln
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ptln
.RB "\(em open the session manager"
.br
.B ptln new
.I <claude|codex|gemini|opencode|goose|antigravity>
.RB [ \-\-worktree
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-keep-going
.IR N ]
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
One binary, two things.
.TP
.B Session manager
Every AI coding session on this machine in one list \(em across engines, directories and git
worktrees. Resume one, fork one into a worktree, split the window, rename, pin, or delete. Sessions
outlive the terminal they were started in, and the manager finds them again after a reboot.
.TP
.B Repo\(hylocal memory
What the team has learned about a repository, kept as one markdown file per fact in
.B memory/
INSIDE that repository. Checking out the code checks out its context; a fact is reviewed in the
same pull request as the change that taught it. There is no server, no account and no second
repository to clone.
.PP
Partyline never commits your repository. A fact is written and left in your working tree for you to
commit with the change it came from.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ptln
Open the session manager. The only argument\(hyless command, because it is the one you run most.
.TP
.B ptln new \fI<tool>\fR [dir] [flags]
Start a new session with an engine, optionally in a new git worktree. Arguments after the engine
are passed through to it untouched.
.TP
.B ptln memory \fI<brief|add|ls|edit|append|rm|where|map|hooks|build|harvest|propose>\fR
The repository's facts. See
.B MEMORY
below.
.TP
.B ptln tmux \fR[\-\-resume|\-\-quit|\-\-print\-conf]
The tmux session host that keeps sessions alive between terminals.
.TP
.B ptln wt \fR[ls|rm \fIbranch\fR|prune]
Git worktrees created for sessions: list them, remove one, or prune the ones whose session is gone.
.TP
.B ptln keepgoing \fR[status|off]
Arm or disarm the keep\(hygoing hook on a session, which answers "continue?" a bounded number of
times so a long task is not stopped by a prompt nobody is there to answer.
.TP
.B ptln llms
The session manager, named explicitly. The bare command does the same.
.TP
.B ptln welcome
The first\(hyrun switchboard: the few things worth doing when you have no sessions yet.
.TP
.B ptln tray
The macOS menu bar companion \(em live session count, and what is waiting on you.
.TP
.B ptln state
A JSON snapshot of this machine's live sessions, for the tray and for scripts.
.TP
.B ptln upgrade
Upgrade in place, the same way this binary was installed (Homebrew or the install script).
.TP
.B ptln man \fR·\fB ptln help \fR·\fB ptln version
This page, the command list, and the build.
.SH MEMORY
A fact is one markdown file with YAML front\(hymatter under
.B memory/
in the repository. One file per fact is deliberate: git merges file ADDITIONS without conflict, so
two people recording facts on two branches never collide.
.TP
.B ptln memory add \fI<kind>\fR "..."
Record something durable. The kinds are
.BR decision ", " constraint ", " contract ", " gotcha " and " question .
.TP
.B ptln memory brief
What a session is told before you type.
.TP
.B ptln memory ls \fR[\-\-all]
Every current fact; \fB\-\-all\fR includes superseded ones.
.TP
.B ptln memory edit \fI<id>\fR "..." \fR·\fB append \fI<id>\fR "..." \fR·\fB rm \fI<id>\fR
Facts are MUTABLE. A claim that turned out wrong is rewritten, not annotated with a correction
filed beside it \(em which is how the previous store ended up briefing an agent with a fact and its
contradiction at the same time. Nothing is lost: the files are in the repo, so
.B git log \-p memory/
is every edit, its author and its date.
.TP
.B ptln memory where
Which directory holds this repository's facts, and how many there are.
.TP
.B ptln memory build \fR[\-\-apply] [\-\-remove\-stale]
Seed a repository that has no facts, and rebuild one whose facts have rotted \(em the same
operation, because the one that runs on a repository people already use is the one that matters.
It records only what it can cite: languages and layout, entry points, build and test commands, CI,
migrations, and the files git shows change most. Anything that would require knowing \fIwhy\fR is
recorded as an open question instead of a guess, because a seed full of confident, subtly wrong
claims poisons every session after it. A dry run by default. Facts you wrote yourself are invisible
to it and are never rewritten or removed; a derived fact whose evidence has vanished is
\fIproposed\fR for removal and only deleted with \fB\-\-remove\-stale\fR.
.TP
.B ptln memory map
Rewrite the summary block in \fBAGENTS.md\fR, \fBCLAUDE.md\fR and \fBGEMINI.md\fR from the current
facts. Every fact change does this anyway; the command is for when they drift \(em a merge that
took someone else's block, or a fact edited by hand in the repo.
.TP
.B ptln memory harvest \fR[\-n] \fR·\fB propose \-\-from \fI<src>\fR
Record what this repository's new commits declare, or a fact mined from another system.
.SS A fact has to earn its place
The test, applied when a fact is written:
.I would this still be true and useful to someone who opens this repo in three months and was not
.IR here ?
Host and machine state, work\(hyitem status, and anything whose subject is not this repository are
refused with the reason. The gate is calibrated against real entries that made a real store feel
like junk \(em "the Mac mini's disk keeps filling up" filed beside interface contracts \(em and is
deliberately narrow, because a gate that blocks good facts is worse than no gate.
.SH ENGINES
The set is closed: claude, codex, gemini, opencode, goose, antigravity.
.PP
The facts are rendered into \fBAGENTS.md\fR, \fBCLAUDE.md\fR and \fBGEMINI.md\fR between managed
markers. That is the floor and it needs nothing: every engine reads its own file at session start,
with no hook, no trust prompt and no integration with partyline, so a checkout carries its context
to engines partyline has never heard of. Your own prose in those files is never touched.
.PP
What a file cannot do is refresh. A session that started on Monday will not see a fact recorded on
Thursday, which is what the hooks add.
.B ptln memory hooks
reports which engines refresh mid\(hysession and which still need a trust grant;
.B \-\-install
writes the hook where it safely can.
.TP
.B Claude
.B UserPromptSubmit
\(em wired per session at launch. No config file and no trust gate.
.TP
.B Codex
.BR UserPromptSubmit .
Trust is granted once in the TUI. Codex disables untrusted hooks \fIsilently\fR \(em
.B codex exec
runs with hooks off and says nothing \(em so partyline reports its trust state as unknown rather
than assuming it, and prints the config for you to paste rather than rewriting a TOML file it
cannot fully parse.
.TP
.B Gemini
.BR BeforeAgent .
Needs the workspace trusted, or \fB\-\-skip\-trust\fR.
.TP
.B Antigravity
.BR PreInvocation .
Needs the directory in \fBtrustedWorkspaces\fR.
.SH SESSION FLAGS
.TP
.B \-\-worktree \fIname\fR
Start the session in a new git worktree on a branch of that name, so two agents editing the same
repository never see each other's half\(hywritten files.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-going \fIN\fR
Answer "continue?" up to N times before stopping and asking you.
.SH IN A SESSION
.B ctrl\-\\
then a key, inside a partyline\(hyhosted session:
.TP
.B w
fork this session into a git worktree
.TP
.B n
new session
.TP
.B g
arm or disarm keep\(hygoing
.TP
.B |
split the window and open a manager in the new pane
.SH IN THE MANAGER
.TP
.B \(rs\(rs⏎
resume the highlighted session
.TP
.B p
pin it to the top of the list
.TP
.B x
delete it. Confirms first, showing its age, worktree, uncommitted files and whether anything else
is live in the same tree. A session that is currently running cannot be deleted \(em close it
first. Deleted sessions move to a trash directory rather than being unlinked.
.TP
.B /
filter
.TP
.B D
open the documentation in a browser
.TP
.B ?
the full key list
.SH FILES
.TP
.B <repo>/memory/*.md
This repository's facts, one per file. Checked in with the code.
.TP
.B <repo>/AGENTS.md\fR, \fBCLAUDE.md\fR, \fBGEMINI.md
The derived summary, inside managed markers. Partyline only creates one of these if the repository
has none, and then only \fBAGENTS.md\fR.
.TP
.B ~/.partyline/
Session index, launcher state, the tmux config partyline generates, and the delete trash.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PARTYLINE_MEMORY_REFRESH=off
Disable the mid\(hysession refresh. The session\(hystart brief still applies.
.TP
.B GEMINI_CLI_TRUST_WORKSPACE
Gemini's own variable, read only to report whether gemini will actually run the hook here.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Open the session manager:
.PP
.nf
    ptln
.fi
.PP
Start a session in an isolated worktree:
.PP
.nf
    ptln new claude \-\-worktree spike\-auth
.fi
.PP
Seed a legacy repository, review, then keep it:
.PP
.nf
    ptln memory build                  # dry run, with the evidence for each fact
    ptln memory build \-\-apply
    git diff memory/                   # they are proposals, not gospel
.fi
.PP
Record something the next person needs, and correct it later:
.PP
.nf
    ptln memory add gotcha "Turbopack rejects a symlinked node_modules; it needs a real directory."
    ptln memory ls
    ptln memory edit 20261017\-2013 "...what turned out to be true instead"
.fi
